  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1230 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nAnother upper-level low digs south towards the AZ/NM/Mexico border \nearly Saturday morning. Models continue to trend towards a deeper \nlow, showing it over northern Mexico and increasing chances \n(generally 40-80%) of rain mainly south of I-20. Though a few \nisolated showers may develop to the north. On Sunday the best rain \nchances (60-80%) become confined to the Big Bend/Presidio \nValley/Lower Trans-Pecos region as the low tracks to the south.\nHowever, exact timing and coverage remains uncertain at this time. \nExactly where the best coverage and rainfall amounts occur strongly \ndepend on the track of the aforementioned upper low. Otherwise, \nincreased cloud cover and rain-cooled air yield high temperatures in \nthe 50s for most on Saturday. Highs in the 50s become confined to \nthe higher terrain Sunday, with 60s elsewhere.\n\nModels show a split jet early next week, with the southern one over \nour area and the other across the northern CONUS. This will prevent \ncold Canadian air from filtering into the region. As a result, near-\nnormal temperatures are expected to return next week, particularly \non Tuesday and Wednesday where weak ridging tries to build over our \narea. Meanwhile, some deterministic models show a few disturbances \nmoving through the flow aloft which could bring some isolated \nshowers next week. Details will be clearer in the coming days.  \n\nGreening",
